Treatment of hyperphosphatemia based on specific interaction between phosphorus and active center Zr(IV) of nano-MOFs
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x
www.rsc.org/
Hyperphosphatemia is closely associated with the occurrence of multiple organ dysfunctions in patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD). The application of phosphorus binders, as an effective clinical approach for such diseases, still suffers from serious side effects. Therefore, development of new phosphorus binders for the treatment of hyperphosphatemia remains great challenge. Herein, we describe a kind of zirconium (Zr(IV))-based nano-MOFs that is well suited for specific adsorption and selectively phosphate's fluorescence sensing, which is based on the particular interaction between Zr(IV) and phosphate. The reduced levels of phosphate were quantitatively monitored by the MOFs-based fluorescence nanosensor. Notably, the MOFs exhibit stronger phosphorus' reductivity than commercially available phosphorus binders, and comparable therapeutic effects in the treatment of mice mode's hypophosphatemia. Hence, the MOF acts as a promising medication for hyperphosphatemia by directly adsorbed phosphorus in blood and offers new perspectives in future MOFs' applications.
## Introduction
Chronic kidney disease, especially renal failure, has dramatically increased in the past years as a serious global public problem to threaten humans. Currently, hemodialysis, as an effective renal failure therapy, still experiencing limitations in the toxins' removal that leads the presence of metabolites in the body. Moreover, hyperphosphatemia, considered to be the common metabolic complications in hemodialysis patients, was found to be the key for raising serum phosphorus levels of chronic kidney disease-mineral and bone disorder (CKD-MB), thereby further increase the occurrence and development of CKD-MB. The main clinical manifestations of CKD-MB are hyperparathyroidism, abnormal mineral and bone metabolism, calcification of blood vessels and other soft tissues. Such disease not only increases the patients' morbidity and seriously influences their life quality, but also increases the incidence of cardiovascular diseases and eventually death. Hence, how to cure hyperphosphatemia becomes quite crucial for the prevention and control of complications in hemodialysis patients, and urgently need to be solved.
The phosphorus binders currently used for the
hyperphosphatemia treatment are aluminum, calcium and iron based binders, as well as \(\mathsf{L a}_{2}(\mathsf{C O}_{3})_{3}\) and etc. However, these binders' side effects and their impact on the patient's prognosis remain a serious concern. Take aluminum-based phosphorus binders for example, its toxicity on the central nervous system, blood system, and bones significantly limit their clinical application. On the other side, La accumulates in many organs such as liver and kidney, which cause adverse reactions like encephalopathy. Therefore, to develop new-type phosphorus-reducing drugs with less side effects are facing severe challenges.
In order to improve the materials' therapeutic effect, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) as a new kind of porous complex, which has been widely used in the fields of biosensing, catalysis, photodynamic therapy due to its controllable synthesis, structural diversity and high specific surface area, has caused our interest. Recently, our group designed nano-MOFs with \(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})\) as the active center to reduce the glutathione levels and increase the concentration of ROS, thus strengthen the photodynamic efficiency and achieve synergistic antitumor effect. In this regard, we hypothesized an Zr (IV)-based nano-MOF with active center prone to the specific phosphorus reactions, which owns the advantages of large surface area, enhanced chemisorption abilities, as well as selectively phosphate fluorescence sensing for monitoring the process. The Zr (IV) as the active center of nano-MOF can specifically bind and absorb in vivo phosphate, thus directly decreasing the blood phosphorus levels and lead to hyperphosphatemia's remission. Notably, compared to commercially available phosphorus binders, the nano-MOF exhibits greatly enhanced decrement of phosphorus levels. At last, hyperphosphatemia mice model with the MOF treatment
further proved less in vivo toxicity and better curing effect. This work provides strong evidence for MOFs as a promising drug for treatment of hyperphosphatemia by directly adsorbed phosphorus in blood, which will further optimized the clinical applications in the future.
## Results and discussion
## Characterization
Typically, porphyrin-inlaid Zr (IV)-based nano-MOFs UiO-66 materials were prepared and characterized. The nano-MOF's mechanical stability was firstly investigated (Fig. 1a), where the PXRD patterns of the nano-MOF treated with PBS were consistent with that of the as-prepared MOFs, which proves the decent crystalline stability in biological environments. By comparison of FTIR absorption of UiO-TCPP and UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}\), identical results were obtained (Fig. S1) which suggests that porphyrin does not affect the MOFs' crystal structure. The morphology and particle size of the MOFs were acquired through dynamic light scattering (DLS), SEM and TEM (Fig. 1b-1d), which demonstrated that the particle fine size control with a uniform distribution of \(120\;\mathsf{n m}\).
## Fluorescence response
The fluorescence response between MOFs and phosphate was studied via two largest fluorescence emission peaks with 440 nm (2-aminoterephthalic acid) and 650 nm (porphyrin), respectively (Fig. S2). By interacting with phosphate, the fluorescence intensitis of MOFs were significantly enhanced due to the charge transfer interruption between the ligand molecule and metal junction of the MOFs, and thus the ligand molecule's fluorescence restored. To further explore the mechanism of fluorescence intensity change caused by MOFs and phosphate, the fluorescence response between MOFs and phosphate in the presence/absence of Zr (IV) ions were carried out (Fig. S3). Interesting, no significant fluorescence change was observed by adding different concentrations of phosphate, attributing to Zr (IV) ions' preferentially interaction with the phosphate, Zr (IV) of MOFs does not interact with phosphate, so there is no obviously changes of fluorescence intensity, which further demonstrated the important role of Zr (IV) of MOFs for identification and fluorescence sensing phosphate.
To thoroughly investigate the interaction between the MOFs and phosphate, the pH concentration and response time were optimized (Fig. S4). The fluorescence responses of the MOFs towards phosphate first increased greatly till \(60\;\mathsf{min}\), and then slow down with time extension, while the plateau was reached by \(120\ \mathsf{min}\). Under optimal conditions, the MOFs' phosphate absorption specificity was investigated by its fluorescence response (Fig. S5). The phosphate was found to enhance the fluorescence intensities of MOFs (435 nm and \(650\ \mathsf{nm}\)) with a linear relationship range of \(0.5{-}105~\upmu\mathsf{M}\) and \(0.5{-}7~\upmu\mathsf{M}\) and the correlation coefficients of 0.9987 and 0.9997, respectively. Based on fluorescence response of the MOFs with phosphate, the material is used for fluorescence monitoring the levels of phosphate in samples.
## Selectivity
The MOFs' phosphate absorption selectivity was studied with the interference of common in vivo small molecular species. Fig. 2a displays that the fluorescence responses are significantly weaker of the ions even with higher concentration than phosphate, representing a high selectivity of the MOFs towards phosphate. By further verification of the relationship between Zr (IV)-based MOFs and phosphate, UV-Vis was carried out and an enhanced absorption at \(420\ \mathsf{nm}\) was obtained after \(1\ \mathsf{h}\) incubation (Fig. S6). This is most probably because phosphate, when binding to the MOFs' network architecture, influence the coordination between the metal center and ligands, thus enhanced the absorption intensity of the porphyrin. UV-Vis was performed to confirm the hypothesis by measuring the concentration of phosphate after incubating with MOFs (Fig. S7), and \(92.4\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mg}\) was achieved demonstrating a significant decrease from phosphate solution, which exhibit significant adsorption capacity for phosphate.
## Adsorption capacity
To improve the biocompatibility of the MOFs, the BSA protein was used to modify the MOFs' surface. The surface charge changes of MOFs before and after BSA's modification were measure via zeta potential. The potential increase from -13.7 \(\mathsf{mV}\) to \(-3.5\ \mathsf{mV}\) (Fig. S8), indicating that the BSA protein was successfully attached onto the surface of the MOFs (BSA-MOFs). Then, the hyperphosphatemia serum model was used for verifying if BSA-MOFs can reduce the phosphate through UV-Vis and ICP emission spectrometer (Fig. S9), and good efficacy were obtained in both characterizations. Subsequently, other commercially available phosphorus binders were compared with MOFs (Fig. 2b), but the MOFs exhibit of obviously better adsorption capacity than all the others. In addition, the MTT test showed the excellent biocompatibility of the MOFs in vivo (Fig. S10). Those results demonstrated the MOFs directly adsorb phosphate, which remarkably reduce the phosphate levels.
## Treatment of hyperphosphatemia in mouse model
Then, a mouse model of chronic renal failure was established using adenine chemical induction. By measuring the physiological indicators such as renal function, blood phosphorus levels, kidney tissue sections, body weight changes, and imaging of mouse kidney tissue in normal and model mice, those results showed that all physiological indicators of model mice were clearly distinguished from normal mice, indicating successful establishment of a renal failure mouse model (Table 1 and Fig. 3, 4). Therefore, the phosphate reducing efficacy by MOFs using the hyperphosphatemia mouse model were rationally designed.
