Next, we tested the efficacy of lowing phosphate levels by MOFs using the hyperphosphatemia mouse model. After 10 days treatment with MOF into the mice's stomach, the physiological indicators were measured through different means. The serum phosphorus value and other indicators in the treated group are identical from those of the control group, indicating that the kidney metabolism of the treated group has mainly recovered to normal (Table 1). To verify if the MOFs can relieve the symptoms of renal failure in mice, weight changes in mice in the both groups were studied, where the treatment group owns slightly faster increase than the model group, which correlates with the hypothesis. As shown in Fig. 4a, the fluorescence images of kidney tissue (model group and therapy group) showed significant differences, suggesting the phosphorus levels in the kidneys have been significantly reduced. By comparing the kidney tissue slides (Fig. 4b), the renal tissue images of the treated group no longer exhibited dilation of the renal tubules compared with the model group, more importantly the treatment group had no pathological abnormalities of the renal tissues.
Finally, the levels of phosphate in the kidneys of the therapy group was measured based on the fluorescence of the nanoMOFs (Fig. 4c and 4d). It can be seen that there was no significant difference in fluorescence intensity, suggesting the phosphate levels between the treated group and the normal group has no significant difference. More importantly, the results measured by fluorescence method were basically identical to that of other methods. Those results confirmed
that the phosphate levels in the treated group were remarkably reduced, and the physiological indicators of the mouse models were basically consistent with normal mouse, indicating that the hyperphosphatemia mouse model was effectively treated by the MOFs. In addition, the in vivo toxicity of organs was tested to prove the biosafety of the MOFs material as a phosphorus binding agent (Fig. S11). Experiments of tissue slides (Heart, Liver, Spleen and Lung, respectively) were performed. Although the nano-MOFs enters the bloodstream via stomach feeding, the images showed the MOFs material has less effect on other organs, which indicated the nano-MOF has less in vivo toxicity.
## Conclusions
In summary, as chronic kidney disease retains high phosphorus blood, we have developed a nano-MOF with Zr (IV) as the active center to reduce the blood phosphorus levels and cure hyperphosphatemia by the direct adsorption of phosphorus. The highly porous MOFs offer significant amount of surface Zr (IV) active centers for interactions, which dramatically reduce the phosphorus concentrations in vivo. By further comparison with in vivo small molecules and other phosphate binder, the MOFs possess high specificity and selectivity on phosphorus reduction. Finally, the hyperphosphatemia mouse model with MOFs treatment shows identical physiological parameters with the control group, representing promising biocompatibility for future applications. The present work provides not only a fluorescence nanosensor for monitoring blood phosphorus levels, but a new approach for capable hyperphosphatemia treatment in clinical therapies.
